clueweb09-enwp03-13-19469.0 Sugar Creek Township, Clinton County, Indiana From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to: navigation, search Sugar Creek Township [Location of Sugar Creek Township in Clinton County] Location of Sugar Creek Township in Clinton County Coordinates: 40ýý13ýýý53ýýýN 86ýý17ýýý12ýýýWýýý / ýýý40.23139ýýN 86.28667ýýWýýý / 40.23139; -86.28667 Country United States State Indiana County Clinton Organized 1841 Government - Type Indiana township Area - Total 25.84 sq mi (66.93 km2) - Land 25.84 sq mi (66.93 km2) - Water 0 sq mi (0 km2) Elevation [1] 919 ft (280 m) Population (2000) - Total 486 - Density 18.81/sq mi (7.26/km2) FIPS code 18-73862[2] GNIS feature ID 453881 Sugar Creek Township is one of fourteen townships in Clinton County, Indiana. As of the 2000 census, its population was 486. The township is named for the stream that flows southwest through it.[3] Contents 1 History 2 Geography 2.1 Unincorporated towns 2.2 Adjacent townships 2.3 Major highways 3
clueweb09-enwp03-13-19469.120 population was 486. The township is named for the stream that flows southwest through it.[3] Contents 1 History 2 Geography 2.1 Unincorporated towns 2.2 Adjacent townships 2.3 Major highways 3 References 4 External links [edit] History The first settler in the area of Sugar Creek Township was Williams Harris, who arrived in 1828, built a cabin and hunted and fished in the wilderness ýýý he was the only white inhabitant for four years, until the arrival of farmer Abner Dunn and his family in 1832, then Merrill Cooper and blacksmith W. V. McKinney in 1835. Though originally slow, settlement in the township increased in the 1840s and by 1850 its population was 477; by 1860 it was 719, in 1870 it was 964, and in 1880 it was 1410. The area's residents petitioned in 1841 for their own township, a request granted by the county commissioners who formed Sugar Creek
